Below is a complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready recipe for putting a scanned 1920‑s map (a JPG) on a **MapLibre GL JS** map, positioning it with the four known corner coordinates, and letting the user turn its opacity on/off (or slide it).  

---

## 1. What you need

| Item | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **The image** – a publicly reachable URL (e.g. `https://mycdn.com/old‑city‑1920.jpg`). | MapLibre loads it with an `<img>` tag, so it must be reachable from the browser. |
| **Four corner coordinates** – in **lon/lat (WGS‑84)** order: top‑left, top‑right, bottom‑right, bottom‑left. | The `image` source expects an array of four `[lon, lat]` pairs that define the quadrilateral where the image is stretched. |
| **MapLibre GL JS** (v2.x or later) loaded on the page. | The library that draws the map and the raster overlay. |
| (Optional) **A UI control** – a checkbox or a range slider. | To let the user change the opacity. |

> **Tip:** If you want the historic map to have transparent background (e.g. to see modern streets underneath), convert the JPG to PNG with an alpha channel first. The code works the same way; only the URL changes.

---

## 2. The core MapLibre code

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Historic Map Overlay – MapLibre</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <!-- MapLibre CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    .opacity-control {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px;
      left: 10px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      padding: 8px 12px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      font-family: sans-serif;
      font-size: 14px;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<div class="opacity-control">
  <label>
    <input type="checkbox" id="toggleOverlay" checked>
    Show 1920 map
  </label>
  <br>
  Opacity:
  <input type="range" id="opacitySlider" min="0" max="100" value="70">
</div>

<!-- MapLibre JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the base map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // A simple OSM raster basemap – replace with your own style if you have one
        osm: {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: [
            'https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
            'https://b.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
            'https://c.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
          ],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'osm',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'osm'
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0], // will be overwritten once the overlay loads
    zoom: 2
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Define the historic image source + layer
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // ----> Replace these with your real data <----
  const historicImageUrl = 'https://mycdn.com/old-city-1920.jpg';

  // Corner coordinates in lon/lat (WGS‑84). Order matters!
  //   [top‑left, top‑right, bottom‑right, bottom‑left]
  const historicCorners = [
    [-122.4525, 37.8044], // top‑left  (west, north)
    [-122.4470, 37.8044], // top‑right (east, north)
    [-122.4470, 37.7990], // bottom‑right (east, south)
    [-122.4525, 37.7990]  // bottom‑left (west, south)
  ];
  // -------------------------------------------------

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 2a – add the image source
    map.addSource('historic-1920', {
      type: 'image',
      url: historicImageUrl,
      coordinates: historicCorners
    });

    // 2b – add a raster layer that draws the image
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'historic-1920-layer',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'historic-1920',
      paint: {
        // Start at 70 % opacity (range 0‑1)
        'raster-opacity': 0.7
      }
    });

    // 2c – zoom the map so the whole image is visible
    const bounds = historicCorners.reduce((b, coord) => b.extend(coord), new maplibregl.LngLatBounds());
    map.fitBounds(bounds, { padding: 20 });
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  UI – toggle visibility & change opacity
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const toggle = document.getElementById('toggleOverlay');
  const slider = document.getElementById('opacitySlider');

  // Helper to set opacity (0‑1)
  function setOpacity(value) {
    const opacity = value / 100; // slider is 0‑100
    map.setPaintProperty('historic-1920-layer', 'raster-opacity', opacity);
  }

  // Checkbox – show / hide the whole layer
  toggle.addEventListener('change', (e) => {
    const visibility = e.target.checked ? 'visible' : 'none';
    map.setLayoutProperty('historic-1920-layer', 'visibility', visibility);
  });

  // Slider – adjust opacity
  slider.addEventListener('input', (e) => {
    setOpacity(e.target.value);
  });

  // Initialise slider value to match the layer's default
  setOpacity(slider.value);
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Map init** | Loads a minimal raster basemap (OSM). Replace the `style` with your own vector style if you have one. |
| **`addSource('historic-1920', {type:'image', …})`** | Registers the scanned map as an **ImageSource**. The `coordinates` array tells MapLibre where each corner of the image belongs on the globe. The order **must be** `[top‑left, top‑right, bottom‑right, bottom‑left]`. |
| **`addLayer({type:'raster', source:'historic-1920', …})`** | Renders the image as a raster layer. The `raster-opacity` paint property controls transparency (0 = invisible, 1 = fully opaque). |
| **`fitBounds`** | After the source is added, we compute a `LngLatBounds` from the four corners and zoom the map so the whole historic map is on screen. |
| **UI controls** | A simple checkbox toggles the layer’s `visibility` layout property. A range slider (0‑100) updates `raster-opacity` in real time. |

---

## 3. Why we use an **ImageSource** (not a TileSource)

* The historic map is a **single raster image** that already contains the whole area you need.  
* `type: 'image'` lets MapLibre stretch that image to any quadrilateral you specify – perfect for old maps that are not perfectly north‑up.  
* No need to generate tiles or a WMTS service unless the image is huge ( > 5 k px on a side). If it is, consider slicing it into tiles and using a `raster` source with a tile URL template.

---

## 4. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Problem | Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------|-----|
| **Image appears in the wrong place / rotated** | Corner order is wrong or coordinates are lat/long swapped. | The `coordinates` array **must** be `[top‑left, top‑right, bottom‑right, bottom‑left]` and each entry is `[lon, lat]`. Double‑check with a small test (e.g., set all four corners to the same point – the image collapses to a dot). |
| **Image looks stretched or skewed** | The four corners are not a true rectangle in Web Mercator (e.g., you used a planar map projection). | For historic maps that were drawn in a *different* projection, you may need to pre‑warp the image to Web Mercator (e.g., with GDAL `gdalwarp`) before using it as an ImageSource. |
| **Opacity control does nothing** | The layer ID is miss‑typed in `setPaintProperty`. | Verify the layer ID (`historic-1920-layer`) matches exactly. |
| **Image never loads (404)** | URL is wrong or CORS blocks the request. | Host the image on a server that sends `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` (or your domain). If you use a CDN, make sure the file is public. |
| **Image is blurry at high zoom** | A low‑resolution JPG is being stretched. | Either (a) use a higher‑resolution scan, or (b) generate raster tiles at multiple zoom levels (e.g., with `gdal2tiles.py`). |
| **MapLibre throws “source type image is not supported”** | Using an older version of MapLibre (< 1.0). | Upgrade to at least v1.13 (the `image` source was added in v1.13). The CDN link above uses v2.4. |

---

## 5. Going a step further (optional)

| Feature | How to add it |
|---------|---------------|
| **Fade‑in/out animation** | Use `map.setPaintProperty('historic-1920-layer', 'raster-opacity', value, {duration: 300});` (MapLibre supports transition options). |
| **Layer ordering** | Add the historic layer **after** the basemap but **before** any labels you want to stay on top. Use `map.moveLayer('historic-1920-layer', 'some-other-layer-id')` if needed. |
| **Persist user choice** | Store the opacity/visibility in `localStorage` and read it on page load. |
| **Multiple historic maps** | Add more `image` sources with different IDs and toggle them independently. |
| **Georeferencing tool** | If you only have a rough estimate of the corners, you can let the user drag the four corner markers (using `map.addControl(new maplibregl.GeolocateControl())` + custom markers) and then export the resulting coordinate array. |

---

## 6. TL;DR – Minimal code snippet

If you already have a MapLibre map (`map`) and the corner array (`corners`) ready, the **bare‑bones** overlay is just:

```js
map.addSource('historic', {
  type: 'image',
  url: 'https://mycdn.com/old-city-1920.jpg',
  coordinates: corners   // [[lon,lat], …] in TL‑TR‑BR‑BL order
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'historic-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'historic',
  paint: { 'raster-opacity': 0.7 }
});
```

Then toggle opacity with:

```js
// 0‑1 value
map.setPaintProperty('historic-layer', 'raster-opacity', newOpacity);
```

