## TL;DR  
Yes – you can display a **MapLibre‑style vector‑tile basemap** inside a Leaflet map.  
The easiest way is to use a small Leaflet plugin that embeds a **MapLibre‑GL** canvas as a single Leaflet layer.  
The most popular choices are:

| Plugin | How it works | When to use it |
|--------|--------------|----------------|
| **`leaflet-maplibre-gl`** (formerly `leaflet-mapbox-gl`) | Wraps a full MapLibre‑GL map as a Leaflet `TileLayer`‑like object. | You want the full power of MapLibre‑GL (style JSON, expressions, 3‑D terrain, etc.) with minimal code changes. |
| **`Leaflet.VectorGrid`** (VectorGrid.Protobuf) | Renders vector‑tile data yourself (GeoJSON/Mapbox Vector Tiles) using Leaflet’s canvas/SVG. | You need fine‑grained control over feature interaction, custom styling per feature, or want to stay 100 % in Leaflet’s rendering pipeline. |

Below you’ll find **step‑by‑step instructions** for both approaches, plus tips, gotchas, and a quick decision guide.

---

## 1️⃣ Quick Decision Guide – Which approach fits you?

| Situation | Recommended plugin |
|-----------|--------------------|
| **You already have a MapLibre style JSON** (or you want to use a public style like OSM‑Bright, MapTiler, etc.) and you just need a basemap layer. | `leaflet-maplibre-gl` |
| **You need per‑feature click/hover handling** (e.g., show a popup for a road, highlight a building) and you want to stay in Leaflet’s event system. | `Leaflet.VectorGrid` |
| **You want 3‑D terrain, sky, or custom GL shaders**. | `leaflet-maplibre-gl` (because it runs a full GL map under the hood). |
| **You are already using Leaflet’s canvas/SVG for other layers** and you want the vector tiles to blend seamlessly with them. | `Leaflet.VectorGrid` |
| **You are okay with adding a second JS library** (MapLibre‑GL) and a small extra canvas element. | `leaflet-maplibre-gl` (the simplest). |

---

## 2️⃣ Adding a MapLibre‑GL style basemap with **`leaflet-maplibre-gl`**

### 2.1 Install the dependencies

You can use a CDN (quick prototype) or npm/yarn (production build).

#### CDN (HTML)

```html
<link
  rel="stylesheet"
  href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"
/>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>

<link
  rel="stylesheet"
  href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css"
/>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet-maplibre-gl@0.0.13/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
```

> **Version note** – At the time of writing the latest stable versions are Leaflet 1.9.x, MapLibre‑GL 4.2.x, and `leaflet-maplibre-gl` 0.0.13. Adjust if newer releases appear.

#### npm / Yarn (recommended for bundlers)

```bash
npm i leaflet maplibre-gl leaflet-maplibre-gl
# or
yarn add leaflet maplibre-gl leaflet-maplibre-gl
```

Then import in your JS/TS:

```js
import L from 'leaflet';
import 'leaflet/dist/leaflet.css';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
import { MapLibreGL } from 'leaflet-maplibre-gl';
```

### 2.2 Create the Leaflet map

```js
// Basic Leaflet map (centered on Berlin, zoom 12)
const map = L.map('map', {
  center: [52.52, 13.405],
  zoom: 12,
  // optional: make the GL canvas the base layer (no default tile layer)
  preferCanvas: false,
});
```

### 2.3 Add the MapLibre‑GL layer

```js
// Example style URL – you can use any public style JSON.
// MapTiler, OpenMapTiles, or your own hosted style works.
const styleUrl = 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY';

// Create the GL layer
const glLayer = new MapLibreGL({
  // Pass the MapLibre‑GL constructor (helps with bundlers)
  maplibre: maplibregl,

  // The style JSON (URL or object)
  style: styleUrl,

  // Optional: set the GL canvas opacity (0‑1)
  opacity: 1,

  // Optional: preserve the map's center/zoom when the GL map loads
  // (Leaflet will sync automatically)
  // See the plugin docs for more sync options.
});

// Add it to the Leaflet map like any other layer
glLayer.addTo(map);
```

That’s it! The MapLibre‑GL canvas now behaves like a Leaflet tile layer:

* **Zoom & pan** are synced automatically.
* **Leaflet controls** (scale bar, attribution, etc.) work as usual.
* **Other Leaflet layers** (markers, GeoJSON, heatmaps) sit **on top** of the GL canvas.

### 2.4 Adding attribution & handling token security

If your style URL requires an API key (e.g., MapTiler, Mapbox), you should:

```js
L.control.attribution({
  prefix: false,
}).addAttribution(
  '&copy; <a href="https://www.maptiler.com/">MapTiler</a> contributors'
).addTo(map);
```

Never hard‑code secret keys in client‑side code; use a **restricted token** (domain‑locked) or a **proxy** that injects the key.

### 2.5 Example: Full HTML page

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Leaflet + MapLibre‑GL basemap</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"/>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css"/>
  <style>
    #map { height: 100vh; width: 100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet-maplibre-gl@0.0.13/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<script>
  const map = L.map('map', {center: [40.7128, -74.0060], zoom: 12});

  const glLayer = new L.MapLibreGL({
    maplibre: maplibregl,
    style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY',
    attributionControl: false   // we’ll add Leaflet’s own attribution later
  }).addTo(map);

  L.control.attribution({
    prefix: false
  }).addAttribution('&copy; <a href="https://www.maptiler.com/">MapTiler</a> contributors')
    .addTo(map);
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open that file in a browser, replace `YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY` with a valid key, and you have a fully functional Leaflet map with a vector‑tile basemap.

